# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Connect-a-roadmap step 4 — see "Connect a new roadmap" in the
# proofstone/proofstone.dev README.
# Copy this JOB into a ROADMAP repo's .github/workflows/links.yml, alongside the
# jobs already there, and copy docs/check_form.py next to it as
# scripts/check_form.py. Both files are the same in every roadmap repo; nothing
# in either is per-repo, so a new roadmap needs no edits, only the copy.
#
# What it is for: this site renders each roadmap README directly and rebuilds
# within seconds of a push to main. The rules that protect that render live
# HERE — content-guard.mjs, check-build.mjs, eleventy.config.mjs — which means
# they run after a merge. Without this job a contributor's pull request can be
# green in the roadmap repo, break the site the moment it lands, and the
# contributor never sees it: the red mark is on a repository they may not even
# know exists.
#
# check_form.py asserts the four things this site depends on — a parseable
# §-section and milestone, a criterion block adjacent to every starred
# milestone, no executable markup, and a map node for every §-section. Its
# patterns are transcribed from this repository's source with the origin named
# at each one; if you change a rule here, change it there in the same commit, or
# the roadmap CI will go green while the site build stays red.
#
# The workflow already runs on pull_request and on push to main, so the job
# needs no `on:` block of its own. No dependencies to install: the script is
# stdlib-only, like the map renderer next to it.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  form:
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    steps:
      - uses: actions/checkout@v4
      - uses: actions/setup-python@v5
        with:
          python-version: "3.12"
      - name: README keeps the shape the site renders
        run: |
          python scripts/check_form.py
